            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="FAW01" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <forename>Joseph</forename>
                        <surname>Fawcett</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1758">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1804-01-24"/>
                    <occupation>Presbyterian minister</occupation>
                    <occupation>poet</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Fawcett was a dissenting minister with extremely fashionable views who preached the Sunday evening lecture at the Old Jewry meeting-house from 1785. He gave up his lecturing in 1795 and moved to a farm 
      in Hertfordshire with his wife (<persName key="FAW02">Charlotte Fawcett, neé French)</persName>, where he concentrated on his poetry. <persName key="WOR01">William Wordsworth</persName> based the character of the Solitary on him in his long poem <title>The Excursion</title>. 
      Godwin considered him one of his 'four principal oral instructors', along with <persName key="COL01">Samuel Taylor Coleridge</persName>, 
      <persName key="HOL01">Thomas Holcroft</persName>, and <persName key="DYS01">George Dyson</persName>. The 1796 list states that they met first in 
    1778.</p>
                        <p>Godwin recorded his death.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="other">
